cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Scholar-Kit: Akademik Makale ve Tez Üretim Kiti for Claude Code"
message: "If you use Scholar-Kit in your research, please cite it."
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: "Türkan"
    given-names: "Mehmet"
repository-code: "https://github.com/mtvrkan/scholar-kit"
url: "https://scholar.mtvrkan.com"
license: MIT
version: 1.0.0
date-released: 2026-08-26
abstract: >-
  An end-to-end academic research, paper and thesis production kit for Claude
  Code, built for Turkish and English scholarly writing as equal first-class
  targets. Specialist subagents handle read-heavy work in their own contexts; a
  deterministic Python tool layer performs the checks that must not depend on
  model judgement; project state lives on disk behind write-protecting hooks.
  Its central discipline is zero-fabrication citation: bibliographic data comes
  only from DOI content negotiation, every empirical claim binds to an evidence
  card, and phase gates block drafting before outline approval, review before
  citation verification passes, and formatting before major issues reach zero.
  Beyond citation existence, the kit checks whether a verified citation is being
  used to say more than its source does (claim-strength ladder), scans for the
  seven AI-research failure modes catalogued by Lu et al. (2026), and measures
  its own deterministic checkers against labelled gold sets on every test run.
keywords:
  - academic-writing
  - literature-review
  - systematic-review
  - citation-verification
  - research-integrity
  - thesis
  - claude-code
  - turkish-academic-writing
# DOI: intentionally absent. A DOI is minted by archiving a tagged release
# (e.g. via Zenodo's GitHub integration); writing one here before that archive
# exists would be a fabricated identifier — precisely the defect class this kit
# is built to prevent. After the first archived release, add:
#   doi: "10.5281/zenodo.XXXXXXX"
# and add the same value to `identifiers:` below.
